  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q1: a `when` registration is PERSISTENT -- its block runs each time the named event occurs, once per occurrence (spec/interaction-events.md:204-209). One handler is registered for the vendor-prefixed event `acme.shake`, which the host then delivers on tick 1 and again on tick 2 during the `wait 2`. The handler fires on BOTH ticks: two handler-start `instruction` events carrying the `when` head's span at 1:1-1:5 (seq 3 and seq 6, spec/interaction-events.md:147-148) and two `print`s. A vendor-prefixed word is required to pin this at all: both standard v0.1 event words are inherently once-per-run -- `\"start\"` is the start of the interactive run and `\"stop\"` a requested stop notification (spec/interaction-events.md:198-202) -- so with `\"start\"` or `\"stop\"` a one-shot and a persistent implementation produce byte-identical streams. Measured against a runtime mutated back to the pre-ruling one-shot rule, this program prints ONCE; the corpus contained no fixture that delivered a named event twice, which is why the undecided rule shipped.",
